[31.398934, "o", "\u001b[2m    r1:c233 check_order(order: parse.Order) -> raised  validate.py:47  51us  calls below: 1\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m      r1:c234 check_sku(sku: str[8]) -> raised  validate.py:22  22us\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n"]
[42.117354, "o", "The run said OK because `sync_orders` drops any order it can't apply without telling anyone, and last night it dropped \r\n5 orders. Their stock was never taken out, so those products show more on hand than the shelves.\r\n\r\n**What the recording shows (`r1`):**\r\n- 26 orders came from the API. 21 passed validation and 5 raised `validate.SkuFormatError` (`r1:e1`–`r1:e5`, raised at \r\n`validate.py:24`).\r\n- All 5 errors were caught by `except Exception` at `job.py:57`. That handler logs a `warning` and moves on to the next\r\n order.\r\n- The default log level is `ERROR` (`job.py:30`), so the warning is never printed. The job then prints `sync finished: \r\nOK` and exits 0 no matter how many orders it skipped.\r\n\r\n**Which orders:** `applied_orders` in the database has no rows for **SO-20521, SO-20526, SO-20530, SO-20533 and \r\nSO-20537**. Each of them is followed by an applied order, so they're gaps, not the end of the batch.\r\n\r\n**Effect on stock:** comparing the database with `data/count.csv`, the stock is too high by:\r\n- KBD-0101: +9\r\n- HDS-0402: +4\r\n- MON-0201: +1\r\n\r\nEvery other SKU matches the count. That's 14 units across the 5 lost orders.\r\n\r\n**Likely cause of the bad SKUs:** The recording only keeps value lengths, not the values. The SKUs in two of the \r\nfailing orders were 9 characters long, where a valid SKU like `KBD-0101` is 8. That points to extra whitespace, and \r\n`parse.py:38` passes `sku=row[\"sku\"]` through as-is while it strips `order_id` and `channel`. The other three were 8 \r\ncharacters but still failed the `AAA-0000` pattern, so probably lowercase letters or a different separator. I couldn't \r\nconfirm the exact values.\r\n\r\n**Recovery:** A rerun won't fix this. The sales API hands each order over only once, and `data/orders.csv` now holds \r\nonly the newer orders. You'll need to get those 5 orders from the sales system (or correct the stock by hand using the \r\ncount) and run them through the job.\r\n\r\n**Suggested code fixes (none made yet):**\r\n1. In `parse.py`, clean up the SKU before validating it: `sku=row[\"sku\"].strip().upper()`.\r\n2. In `job.py`, keep a list of skipped orders. Report them at `ERROR` level, or exit non-zero when any were skipped, so\r\n `OK` means every order was applied. Also, `except Exception` hides more than validation errors. Narrowing it to \r\n`ValidationError` and `UnknownSkuError` would let a real bug stop the job.\r\n\r\nDo you want me to make these changes?\r\n\r\n"]
[45.666097, "o", "\u001b[2m-- end of the run: 7 tool calls, 45 s --\u001b[0m\r\n"]
